        "problem": "If \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$, then ${P}^{6}=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${P}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{2} & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${P}^{2}=P,$ so $P$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${P}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{6} & ={P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\cdot P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2}\\cdot P \\\\  & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{P}^{6}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
